''Every page-side reader of the opened key''
* Direct readers in `browser-custody.ts`: `readKeystoreRowHeld` `:383`, `writeKeystoreRowHeld` `:412`, `withCustodyRows().delete` via `heldOrRefuse` `:437,587` (bind `:595`, unbind `:613`), and `settleClearRows` (`:552,581`).
* Indirect readers of the opened ''rows'' (seeds in page memory):
** `browser-vessel-identity.ts:202,207,219,224,366,377`
** `browser-face-atom.ts:35,40,87,98`
** `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:39,52`
** `browser-face-anchor.ts:13,17`
** `browser-face-presentation.ts:102`
** `browser-fleet-join.ts:85,98,110,153,163,211`
** `browser-walk.ts:52,53,70`

''The ask'' is a page `<form>` in `askCustody` (`main.ts:115-142`). Its call sites are boot `:399`, fleet join `:303` and persona-mint (`open-browser-vessel.ts:1592-1593`, via `main.ts:408`). Only the passphrase and paper kinds exist (`browser-custody.ts:42-43`).

''The device key''
* Generated ''extractable'' (`browser-vessel-identity.ts:183-193`), exported to JWK, then kept as hex `{verifyingKey, signingKey}` (`:77-82`).
* Rests at IDB `lares:vessel/keystore/vessel-key` (`:45,216-227`), in the clear until a slot binds (`browser-custody.ts:405-408`).
* Signs through <code>&#64;noble/ed25519</code> on the page: `browser-fleet-join.ts:24,103`, `browser-walk.ts:15`, `browser-face-presentation.ts:25`, `browser-handle-verbs.ts:17`.
* The PRF wrap (`seed-wrap-prf.ts`) has ''no caller''.

''Secrets that cross a port today.'' The daemon `manifest` built on the page carries `daemonAuth.atom.seed` (hex of the worn atom seed), `faceAddressSecret` and `veilArchiveBytes` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:1324-1359,1335-1338,1443`; built in `open-browser-daemon-vm.ts:121-131`). All of them were read from custody rows under the unlock (`:1206,1213`).

''⚑ The holder carries unlock-derived secrets.'' Under `?holder`, that manifest flows ''through the SharedWorker holder into an island running in the holder's realm'' (`shared-holder-core.ts:494-528`, `shared-holder.worker.ts:26-44`). The ruling reads "the shared holder still carries no unlock". The tree key never goes there, but the seeds it opened do. Separately, `?holder` selects a topology by URL query, the same class as the refused `?glass=`.

''Stores at rest that nothing seals''
* page `${idbName}:repo` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:565`)
* island stores (`browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:39-41`)
* the edge ledger (`daemon-island-start.ts:17`)
* the holder's house repo (`shared-holder.worker.ts:61`)

''Clear rows'' are allowed before a slot only for `vessel-key` and `veil-archive-g*` (`browser-custody.ts:61,205-224,405-408`). They get sealed and settled at first seal and at every unlock (`:511-535`). Clear goes back to clear rows (`:645-687,707-732`).

''Stale headers owed'' (ruling 8): `seed-wrap-prf.ts:5-11`; `secure-context.mem` #/custody-leans "What the code holds"; `phone-seat.ts` `seedRestStatus(undefined)` ("seed: cleartext at rest", per the memo update of 2026-10-29).

!!! B1. The move: custody into the tab's own daemon worker

''The end-state.'' The daemon island worker (dedicated, one per tab, spawned at `worker-handle.ts:39`) becomes the ''vessel'': the slot tree, the opened tree key, every custody row, every signer. The page keeps a surface, a port, the ask's DOM, and nothing it opened.

''Build''
# A `custody` door on the daemon worker. These kinds join `island-protocol.ts`'s vessel↔island unions (`:705-733` and `holder-routes.ts:6-45`):
#* `custody:status` → `{state: none|clear|sealed|unsettled, slots:[kinds]}`
#* `custody:open{route}` → `opened` | `refused:<why>`
#* `custody:seal{route}`, `custody:bind`, `custody:unbind`, `custody:lock`, `custody:clear`
#* `custody:row-read{row}` and `custody:row-write`, ''for the worker's own use only''. No page caller exists, and a test pins that.
# Move `browser-custody.ts`, its `CustodyIo` and `keyslot` calls, and `settleClearRows` into the worker's import graph. The page imports ''no'' `browser-custody` export. A census test greps the page bundle's graph.
# ''The daemon manifest stops carrying secrets.'' The worker reads the atom seed, `faceAddressSecret` and the veil archive itself, under its own opened key. `daemonAuth` loses `atom.seed`, `faceAddressSecret` and `veilArchiveBytes` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:1335-1338`).
# ''The per-tab ask stands.'' Each tab's worker opens its own tree key. Nothing posts the key or any opened row to another tab, the BroadcastChannel bus or the holder.

''What crosses the port instead of key bytes''
* the route material, ''once'', page → worker (see B4);
* custody ''verdicts'' (`opened`, `refused:<why>`, `status`);
* ''signatures'', never signers: `sign{purpose, bytes}` → `{signature}`. Purposes come from a closed set, and each act's whole bytes are composed ''inside'' the worker (see B2), never chosen by the page;
* public keys and addresses.

''Tests''
* `custody-key-never-in-page` (Playwright): after unlock, a heap snapshot of the ''page'' realm (`page.evaluate` + CDP `HeapProfiler`, Chromium) holds no 32-byte buffer equal to the tree key. The worker receives the key's fingerprint through a test-only door that answers `sha256(key)` and nothing else. ''Control:'' run the same probe today, before B1, and it finds the key in the page heap. Run this control first.
* `page-imports-no-custody`: a bundle-graph census of `lararium-web`'s page entry reads zero imports of `browser-custody`, `keyslot` or `tree-key`. ''Control:'' the census run on today's graph lists them.
* `manifest-carries-no-secret`: the posted `manifest` holds no field named `seed`, `faceAddressSecret` or `veilArchiveBytes`. ''Control:'' today's manifest holds all three.
* `per-tab-ask-holds`: two tabs, and the second tab asks again. No message on the bus or holder carries 32 high-entropy bytes. ''Control:'' an entropy probe flags a planted key.

!!! B2. Signers into the worker; the device key non-extractable in memory and sealed at rest

''⚑ Two readings of "device key non-extractable"'', measured in `project_browser_key_custody` §②:
* ''(i) Rest a non-extractable `CryptoKey` handle in IndexedDB.''
** It persists as ''raw seed bytes on disk'' in Chromium and Firefox (measured).
** ''Any same-origin script can `get` the handle and `sign` with it'': a live signing oracle. A person's plugin on the mirror page is same-origin.
** `wrapKey` throws on it, so it ''cannot be sealed'' under the tree key.
** So reading (i) contradicts "no clear rows under the full mirror" and "no key on a surface". The surface's realm can reach the handle through origin storage.
* ''(ii) Rest the seed sealed (`sealed/vessel-key`, which already exists after a slot binds, `browser-custody.ts:311-323`), and import it `extractable:false` into the **worker's memory'' at each unlock.**
** It is non-extractable in every live realm, sealed at rest, and leaves no oracle in storage.
** It loses the "paper-free, device-bound" property, which §⑤ says costs nothing for the device key.
* ''Recommendation: (ii).'' It is the only reading that satisfies rulings (6), (7) and "no key on a surface" together. What overturns it: the operator naming device-binding against profile-copy as the goal. Then (i) stands, and its oracle residual gets written down.

''Build''
# In the worker: `importKey('pkcs8', 302e020100300506032b657004220420‖seed32, {name:'Ed25519'}, false, ['sign'])`. That is the RFC 8410 prefix from §⑨. Zero the seed buffer after the import.
# Every page signing site becomes a worker act whose bytes the worker composes:
#* `browser-fleet-join.ts:103`
#* `browser-walk.ts` (`:15` and its callers)
#* `browser-face-presentation.ts:25`
#* `browser-handle-verbs.ts:17`
#* `auth-wire.ts:219` `ed25519SignerFromSeed`
#* `device-delegation.ts:138`
#* `oracle-substrate.ts:135`
#* `boot-invite-burn.ts:81`

These are the §⑨ list, re-anchored before editing. The seam already exists (`AdmitSigner = (bytes) => Promise<string>`, `persona-admit.ts:68`). It moves into the worker, and the page receives signatures.
# ''Persona roots stay seeds'' (SLIP-0010 needs bytes; `secure-context.mem` #/two-key-classes). They are read and derived ''inside the worker'' only, and rest sealed (already ruled).
# ''Test hazard:'' Safari's Ed25519 signatures come out randomized, so no test may compare a WebCrypto signature byte-for-byte with noble's (§⑨.6). Tests verify signatures; they never compare bytes.
# Platform support: Ed25519 WebCrypto ships in Chrome/Edge 137, Firefox 129 and Safari 17 (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SubtleCrypto/sign#ed25519, via §①; mdn browser-compat-data). The secure context is required (https://w3c.github.io/webcrypto/).

''Tests''
* `device-key-opaque-in-worker`: the worker's handle reports `extractable === false`, and `exportKey` throws `InvalidAccessError`. ''Control:'' today's keygen at `browser-vessel-identity.ts:186` holds `extractable:true`.
* `device-key-no-handle-at-rest`: after lock, no IDB object store holds a `CryptoKey`, which a structured-clone walk of every store checks. ''Control:'' plant one and the walk finds it.
* `page-cannot-sign`: the page bundle holds no <code>&#64;noble/ed25519</code> import. A page-side `sign{purpose:"arbitrary"}` refuses `purpose-unknown`. ''Control:'' `sign{purpose:"fleet-join"}` from the worker's own flow verifies.

!!! B3. No clear row under the full mirror; the holder re-cut

''Build''
# ''Sequence:'' the mirror's ''plugin gate'' (C3) reads `custody:status === sealed && settled`. A vessel with `clear` or `unsettled` rows stands the mirror with core and house plugins only, plus an alert reading `plugins-await-seal`.
# ''Clear stays a person's act'' (`clearBrowserCustody`, `:645-687`). Taking it closes the plugin gate in this tab and in every tab that next reads status.
# ''The `?holder` road'' (⚑ B0) is re-cut to the ruling:
#* The holder relays ''ciphertext and store coordination only''.
#* No island that holds an opened key or seed runs in the holder's realm.
#* The query flag ''burns''. Topology is measured or ruled, never read from a URL, as with `?glass=`.
#* If the operator wants the holder topology kept, it becomes a capability-measured choice under the same rule.
#* Until ruled, B3 deletes the flag and keeps the dedicated-worker topology, which is the shipped default.

''Tests''
* `mirror-plugins-wait-on-seal`: with a clear `vessel-key` row, a person's plugin does not load into the mirror and the alert names `plugins-await-seal`. ''Control:'' a sealed and settled vessel loads it.
* `holder-carries-no-secret`: if the holder topology stays, an entropy census over every `MessagePort` frame through the holder finds no seed. ''Control:'' today's manifest trips it.

!!! B4. Where the person types the secret: the unlock ceremony

''The problem.'' The surface owns the screen. A person's plugin, running in the mirror on the page realm, can paint a perfect copy of the house's ask and read what the person types into it.

''Platform facts''
# ''No worker owns a UI.'' `WorkerNavigator` carries no `credentials` member. Its properties are `appCodeName` … `userAgentData`, plus `locks`, `permissions`, `storage` and `serviceWorker`, but not `credentials` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WorkerNavigator, read 2026-10-29). Workers hold no DOM (https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html). So ''WebAuthn runs only from a Window'', and a "worker-owned ceremony the page can't fake" ''does not exist on the web platform.''
# ''`<dialog>.showModal()` paints in the top layer'', but the page's own script controls that layer (https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#the-dialog-element). It gives stacking order, not authority.
# ''WebAuthn's prompt'' is drawn by the browser or OS, so the PIN or biometric never enters web content.
#* Its assertion and PRF output land in the ''calling Window's JS'' (W3C WebAuthn L3 §10.1.4, https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/#prf-extension, via `session-unlock-2026.mem`).
#* Any same-origin script can call `navigator.credentials.get` for the same rpId with the same salt. The salt is readable from origin storage.
#* Default allowlists are `self` for `publickey-credentials-get` and `-create`. A cross-origin iframe needs `allow=`, and `create()` there also needs transient activation (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Permissions-Policy/publickey-credentials-create; …/publickey-credentials-get; both marked "Limited availability". Per-browser versions UNVERIFIED, since the compat table did not render).
#* ''PRF support:'' Chrome 132 (iCloud Keychain) / 147 (Windows create); Safari 18 platform, 26.4 for create and get; Firefox 139, Windows Hello 148 (`device-capabilities-2026.mem:93`, house-sourced). Cross-device PRF stays unmeasured (`secure-context.mem` #/custody-leans).
# ''Chrome storage partitioning'' (on since Chrome 115) keys storage by origin + top-level site + an ancestor bit. A fully same-site chain stays unpartitioned. IndexedDB, Web Storage, OPFS, Storage Buckets, BroadcastChannel, SharedWorker and Web Locks all partition (https://privacysandbox.google.com/cookies/storage-partitioning, read 2026-10-29).
# ''WebKit:'' "Third-party LocalStorage and IndexedDB are partitioned per first-party website and also made ''ephemeral''", which the page defines as storage that "does not persist to disk and goes away with the application". ITP deletes all script-writeable storage after 7 days without interaction, and "the first-party domain of home screen web applications is exempt" (https://webkit.org/tracking-prevention/, read 2026-10-29).
#* ''WebKit bug 225297 (NEW, open):'' a same-eTLD+1 subdomain iframe also "gets a unique partition" (https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=225297).
#* One commenter there says such a frame gets "partitioned + ephemeral" storage. That claim is ''UNVERIFIED'', as a question in the thread rather than a finding.
# ''Firefox State Partitioning'' keys by top-level site. A Storage Access grant lasts 30 days (https://hacks.mozilla.org/2021/02/introducing-state-partitioning/, via out.md; ''UNVERIFIED'' this pass).
# ''A sibling subdomain cannot reach another origin's DOM'' unless both opt in through `document.domain`, which origin-keyed agent clusters disable (`Origin-Agent-Cluster: ?1`; https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/domain, ''UNVERIFIED'' this pass).

''The options, weighed''

| Shape | Where the secret is typed | Can a live plugin harvest it? | Storage under partitioning / ITP | Against the rulings |h
| ''W. A worker-owned ceremony'' | — | — | — | ''Does not exist'' (facts 1–2) |
| ''D. A house `<dialog>` on the app origin'' | page DOM | yes: a fake dialog, or a hook on `HTMLInputElement` | first-party; fine | allowed, but the surface paints the ask, so it fails "a ceremony the surface cannot paint" |
| ''P. WebAuthn/PRF on the app origin's rpId'' | the OS/browser prompt; nothing typed into web content | ''partly'': a plugin can call `credentials.get` itself and receive the PRF output if the person approves a real OS prompt | first-party; fine | the secret never enters web content; the residual is a deceived approval |
| ''K-frame. A custody origin in a cross-origin iframe'' (Privy's shape) | the custody frame, or WebAuthn under the custody rpId | a plugin cannot read the frame's DOM or claim its rpId, ''but'' can paint a lookalike //beside// the frame, and nothing in browser chrome tells the two apart | ⚑ ''WebKit: third-party IndexedDB stays ephemeral'' (fact 5). A cross-site custody frame ''loses its slot tree at session end on Safari'', and a same-site sibling still partitions (225297). Chrome cross-site partitions too, but consistently under one top-level site | ⚑ conflicts with RECOVERY and with the iOS seat, the phone being a family's main surface (`device-capabilities-2026.mem:137`) |
| ''K-popup. A custody origin opened top-level'' | the custody origin's own window, with the address bar naming it | phishing only (a lookalike domain); the origin is real browser chrome | first-party for the custody origin, but top-level storage ''differs'' from any framed copy (fact 4) and runs on Safari's 7-day clock unless used. ''UNVERIFIED:'' how a Home Screen app on iOS opens a popup (an in-app browser view with separate storage?) | the result must reach the app's worker ''through the app page'' (the opener), so it rides encrypted to a worker key. A live plugin can MITM the worker's key by using the device key as an oracle, unless the ask runs before any plugin (see R). Costs a second origin (DNS-01 and TLS, `secure-context.mem` #/why-dns-01) |
| ''S. The surface in a sandboxed / cross-origin iframe; vessel and ask top-level'' (lean B of the lane kit) | the top-level house page | ''no'': the platform walls the plugin realm off from the vessel origin's storage and DOM | the vessel origin stays first-party everywhere; the mirror needs no storage | ⚑ ''the operator ruled Mu'' on the iframe menu ("not as user friendly") |

''⚑ The conflict to rule.'' "An unlock ceremony the surface cannot paint" holds by platform only in two shapes:
* shape ''S'' (the surface in another realm), which the operator ruled Mu on;
* a ''second origin'', whose framed variant WebKit's ephemeral third-party storage breaks on Safari.

''Within one origin, no ceremony exists that a person's plugin cannot imitate.'' The platform walls realms apart, not pixels.

''Recommended shape: R, "the ask lives in a clean realm, and its daily secret never enters web content."''

# ''Clean realm.'' The ask runs only in a page realm where ''no person's code has ever run'':
#* The page holds a one-way `realmTainted` latch, set at the first person's-plugin boot. The ask refuses in a tainted realm.
#* ''Lock and re-ask'' go through a ''navigation'' (`location.reload()` under the unload hold) to a fresh realm, and the mirror boots only after the worker answers `opened`.
#* So the legitimate ask ''never appears over a painted wiki''. That gives the person one rule to check: //"if your wiki shows, any ask is fake."// The house writes this in plain words beside the ask.
# ''The daily secret is a passkey (P), held as one keyslot'' (the `custody-leans` condition: a passkey opens ONE keyslot and names no person).
#* The page calls `navigator.credentials.get` with PRF in the clean realm, posts the 32-byte output to the worker ''once'', and zeroes its buffer.
#* The worker derives the slot's KEK and opens the tree key.
#* The transit is named: the PRF output exists in the clean page realm for one task and never rests.
# ''Passphrase and paper stay'' as the recovery slots, as ruled. They are typed only in the clean realm (shape D, sequenced), with the plain-words rule beside them. Where PRF reads absent (measured, never assumed: `device-capabilities-2026.mem:153` names Firefox-on-Android gaps), the passphrase serves as the daily route under the same clean-realm rule.
# ''The opened key lives in the dedicated worker (B1).'' The shared holder carries no unlock (B3). The per-tab ask stands (each tab's fresh realm asks).

''Why R reads isomorphic by intent''
* Custody reads by ''capability'': "the realm that opens keys runs no person's code, and the ask runs where no person's code has run."
** On the node box, that realm is the node process, and the browser holds a port.
** On the anon-device vessel, it is the dedicated worker plus a clean ask.
** On a fleet, it is each vessel's own.
* No host check decides it. The vessel's grant says which custody capability it offers.

''What R leaves open, named for the record''
* ''(a) A deceived approval.'' A live plugin can trigger a ''real'' passkey prompt for the app's rpId (fact 3) and read the PRF output if the person approves it while the wiki is painted. The plain-words rule is the only guard.
* ''(b) A fake passphrase box'' painted over the wiki. Same guard.
* ''(c) The served-page hole.'' Whoever serves the code owns everything (§③). No shape cures this, and R leaves it as it stands.

''What overturns R''
* ''The operator lifts the Mu for the custody path alone (shape S).'' The platform then enforces what R leaves to a plain-words rule. That costs the iframe UX the operator named, and re-measures G7.
* ''A measured Safari fact:'' a same-site sibling-origin frame's IndexedDB ''persists'' (bug 225297's partition without ephemerality). Then K-frame survives Safari, and a custody origin becomes viable as a phishing cure for typed secrets. That needs a second origin served on every setup (Q-B3 of out.md).
* ''Measured PRF breakage'' on the phone seat. The passphrase then becomes the daily route everywhere, and residual (b) widens.

''Tests (B4)''
* `ask-refuses-in-tainted-realm`: after the mirror boots one person's plugin, `custody:open` called from that realm refuses `realm-tainted`, and the lock path navigates. ''Control:'' a fresh realm's ask opens.
* `lock-reloads-the-realm`: `custody:lock` from the surface ends in a navigation (Playwright `waitForNavigation`), and the new realm shows no mirror before `opened`. ''Control:'' the old realm's `window` marker is gone.
* `prf-output-never-rests`: after unlock, no page-heap buffer equals the PRF output (heap probe as in B1). ''Control:'' a deliberately retained copy is found.
* `passkey-slot-binds-and-opens` (virtual authenticator, Chromium CDP `WebAuthn.addVirtualAuthenticator` with PRF): bind a PRF slot beside a passphrase slot, open by either one, and see that refusing one destroys nothing. ''Control:'' a wrong PRF salt refuses `slot-refused`. The slot tree today builds only passphrase and paper (`secure-context.mem` #/custody-leans, "What the code holds"), so the passkey kind lands in `lararium-mesh/src/keyslot.ts` with its own vectors.

---

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/c-the-gate-sealed-store-steps-2-and-3-for-the-br`>>

!! C. The gate: sealed-store steps 2 and 3 for the browser store

''Ruled:'' the full mirror runs people's plugins only after steps 2 and 3 land for the browser store.

''What the browser path owes'', taken from `sealed-store.mem` #/owed ("the browser shore": "the browser's island still owes a rest for this store and a caller that seats its holder") and `stable-founding-road.mem:401-403`:

| Step | Row the browser owes | Node twin to mirror | Depends on |h
| 2 | ''C1a. The browser rest:'' lockboxes, signed records and chunk-index rows at rest, by names their own bytes derive, written whole and once. IndexedDB or OPFS sits behind the `sealed-store-rest.ts` port | `packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-store-fs.ts` | B1 (the worker owns storage I/O) |
| 2 | ''C1b. The caller that seats the holder:'' the browser daemon island stands `content-key-holder` beside its Keyhive identities once `custody:open` answers, over its own `sealed/` and `cad/` tiers. It answers the `sealed-*` verbs and refuses each by name when no identity stands | `node-daemon-island.ts`, `sealed-store-island.ts`, `content-key-seat.ts` | B1 and B2 (the holder lives where the Keyhive seat and the opened key live) |
| 2 | ''C1c. The outbox key's row and hand-off'', under the browser's S key (the tree key) | `custody#/what-the-code-holds` | C1b |
| 2 | ''C1d. The Archive writer'' rides the worker's slot tree (`browser-custody.ts` `CustodyIo`, now worker-side) | — | B1 |
| 2 | ''C1e. The session signer.'' Shared with node: "no content key mints on a production vessel until a production path opens a session signer" (`sealed-store.mem` #/what-the-code-holds). ''The browser cannot finish step 2 alone.'' It waits on O1 / the wire cutover's session open (`train-s3-launch.mem:31`) | — | ⚑ cross-lane blocker |
| 3 | ''C2a. Sealed repos.'' Each Automerge store value seals as a `change` or `checkpoint` value through the holder: the island stores (`browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:39-41`), the edge ledger (`daemon-island-start.ts:17`) and, if kept, the holder's house repo (`shared-holder.worker.ts:61`). ''The page repo `${idbName}:repo` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:565`) leaves the page entirely'', since page never a vessel means the page holds no repo | `train-s3-launch` walk 3 | C1b, C1e |
| 3 | ''C2b. Face-checked hydration:'' every fetch, write, catalog lookup and content read in the worker is checked against the session's face. A wrong face learns no change and no catalog name, even when it holds the key | `train-s3-launch` walk 2 | C2a; O1's verified face |
| 3 | ''C2c. `freshBase` from the browser document store'', cut before any from-now change seals | `content-key-holder.ts` port | C2a |
| 3 | ''C2d. Persona planes rest sealed'' (`selves.carry` stops refusing on the browser) | `persona-selves-plane.ts` | C2a |
| 3 | ''C2e. The re-seal record'' at a roll (ruled; lands with step 3) | `stable-founding-road.mem:228` | C2a |

''The order'' (no "a now, b later"; each row lands in its end-state): B1 → B2 → C1a, C1d → C1b → C1c → [O1 session signer] → C2a → C2b, C2c → C2d, C2e → ''C3''.

''C3. The plugin gate opens.'' The mirror's boot reads one capability off the port grant: `sealedRest: true`. The vessel sets it only when C2a and C2b stand and custody reads `sealed && settled`. With it, person's plugins join the preloads, after the A3 census. Without it, the mirror boots core and house plugins and shows `plugins-await-seal`.
* It is a field of `port:open`'s grant (MIRROR DEFAULT: "the handle = the D2 port grant"), never a URL value and never a stored flag.

''C tests''
* `browser-holder-refuses-unadmitted-port`: row `B1` of `session-unlock-2026.mem`. An unadmitted port asking for a sealed body refuses by name. ''Control:'' the admitted session's port opens it.
* `browser-repo-rests-sealed`: after an edit, every IDB value in the island stores parses as a sealed value (step-1 format) and none contains the edit's text. ''Control:'' today's stores hold the text in the clear. Run this first.
* `browser-wrong-face-learns-nothing`: two faces on one vessel. Face B, even holding the content key, gets no change and no catalog name for face A's bag. ''Control:'' face A reads it.
* `plugin-gate-reads-the-grant`: without `sealedRest`, no person's plugin loads. With it, the plugin loads. No URL or storage value changes the outcome, and a census holds the glass sources to the grant field.

