<<~ ahu slot=`#/questions-only-the-operator-can-rule`>>

!! Questions only the operator can rule

# ''Q-A1. Refuse the plugin or the mirror.'' A person's syncadaptor or saver plugin: A3 reads "refused at mount by name" as dropping ''the plugin'', alert named, mirror standing. The other reading refuses ''the mirror'' (`none:plugin-syncadaptor`).
# ''Q-A2. The plugin library door.'' `browser-messaging` disabled means `tm-load-plugin-library` (a web2 iframe fetch) refuses by name. Does the house owe a Web3 plugin-library road (an offering through `nexus publish`) as the people-customize path?
# ''Q-B1 ⚑. The ceremony boundary.'' Within one origin, no ceremony exists that a live plugin cannot imitate (facts 1–3). Choose one:
#* ''R'', the recommendation: clean-realm ask, passkey daily, passphrase and paper recovery, with the residuals named;
#* lift the Mu for the custody path alone (''S'', platform-enforced);
#* a ''custody origin'' (K), whose framed variant WebKit's ephemeral third-party storage breaks on Safari.
# ''Q-B2 ⚑. "Device key non-extractable".'' Reading (ii), the recommendation: sealed at rest and an opaque handle in worker memory per unlock. Or reading (i): a persisted opaque handle, which any same-origin script can use as a signing oracle and which can never be sealed.
# ''Q-B3 ⚑. The `?holder` topology.'' It carries unlock-derived seeds through the shared holder and is selected by URL query. B3 burns the flag and keeps dedicated workers. Does the holder topology return later as a measured capability carrying ciphertext only?
# ''Q-C1 ⚑. The browser seal and O1.'' The browser's steps 2 and 3 wait on the production session signer (O1), the same blocker node has. Does the plugin gate stay closed until O1 lands, or does the operator name an order that brings O1 forward?

